WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.440
- Now, I just explained why
Bill Cosby is going free

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:03.800
because the Pennsylvania Supreme Court

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.980
thinks that his Fifth
Amendment rights were violated,

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.150
but that doesn't mean that
the district attorney,

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.370
Bruce Castor, didn't royally screw up.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.010
The original deal not to prosecute Cosby

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:13.880
was to get him to testify
in the civil suit.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.410
If Cosby wasn't going to be prosecuted,

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:16.660
he could waive his Fifth Amendment rights

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.120
and give deposition testimony,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.970
which is under oath in the civil suit.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.980
But the Fifth Amendment is
different in a civil context.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.530
In a criminal trial, you can
never be forced to testify,

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.100
but, in a civil trial, you can say

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.010
you're not going to testify,

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:28.560
but the judge can take
a negative inference.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:30.810
So, if that had happened
in the civil suit,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.240
the victim's attorney could
have tried to depose Cosby,

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.520
he would have taken his
Fifth Amendment right

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:35.353
and then the judge would say,

00:00:35.353 --> 00:00:36.747
"Well, since you're not going to testify,

00:00:36.747 --> 00:00:38.017
"we're going to take a negative inference

00:00:38.017 --> 00:00:40.077
"and assume that you
probably did the things

00:00:40.077 --> 00:00:41.010
"that you were accused of."

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.290
And, on top of that, it's not like Cosby

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:43.570
is going to admit guilt

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:45.680
freed from any chance
of criminal prosecution.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.110
He put on his defense in the civil case,

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:51.610
he tried to prevent it
from being successful.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:53.930
So the Pennsylvania Supreme
Court's decision makes sense,

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.700
but Bruce Castor's decision
was a boneheaded move

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.300
that probably didn't even
help the victim that much.

